  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does Slack strip metadata from uploaded PDFs?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Slack stores the file object more or less as uploaded, including its Info dictionary and XMP metadata. Slack generates a preview thumbnail and extracts text for search indexing, but it does not remove or alter the original metadata fields inside the file itself."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can coworkers see who created a PDF from Slack search?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Anyone with access to a channel can generally download the file and inspect its properties directly, and the Author, Company, and Producer fields travel with the file wherever it goes. Slack's own search interface does not typically surface metadata fields as searchable text, but the file itself remains fully intact for anyone who opens its properties."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does Microsoft Teams generate link previews for PDFs shared in channels?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Teams generates an inline preview card for files shared in a channel or chat, which can include a thumbnail rendered from the first page. That thumbnail is generated from the file's actual page content, meaning any visible information on that page — not just metadata — appears in the preview automatically, before anyone opens the full document."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is email attachment metadata visible to external recipients?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. Standard email attachments are not modified in transit by most mail servers, so a PDF's metadata reaches the recipient exactly as it left the sender. This applies whether the recipient is internal or external, and most email clients do not display metadata by default, which is part of why it goes unnoticed."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does deleting a Slack message remove the file from search index and legal holds?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Not necessarily. Organizations on paid Slack plans can enable data retention and compliance export policies that preserve messages and files independently of user-initiated deletion, and files under an active legal hold are typically retained regardless of what a user deletes from their own view. Deletion removes visibility, not necessarily the underlying record."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can screenshots or PDF previews leak metadata even if I don't share the raw file?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A plain image screenshot of a PDF page does not carry the PDF's Info dictionary or XMP fields, since those are file-format properties rather than visual content. However, the screenshot image itself can carry its own metadata, such as device or location data from a phone camera, and the visible page content in the screenshot can still expose the same information a full metadata field would."}
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

