    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-section-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does Slack strip metadata from uploaded PDFs?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No. Slack stores the file object more or less as uploaded, including its Info dictionary and XMP metadata. Slack generates a preview thumbnail and extracts text for search indexing, but it does not remove or alter the original metadata fields inside the file itself.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can coworkers see who created a PDF from Slack search?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Anyone with access to a channel can generally download the file and inspect its properties directly, and the Author, Company, and Producer fields travel with the file wherever it goes. Slack's own search interface does not typically surface metadata fields as searchable text, but the file itself remains fully intact for anyone who opens its properties.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does Microsoft Teams generate link previews for PDFs shared in channels?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Teams generates an inline preview card for files shared in a channel or chat, which can include a thumbnail rendered from the first page. That thumbnail is generated from the file's actual page content, meaning any visible information on that page appears in the preview automatically, before anyone opens the full document.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Is email attachment metadata visible to external recipients?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Yes. Standard email attachments are not modified in transit by most mail servers, so a PDF's metadata reaches the recipient exactly as it left the sender. This applies whether the recipient is internal or external, and most email clients don't display metadata by default, which is part of why it goes unnoticed.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does deleting a Slack message remove the file from search index and legal holds?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Not necessarily. Organizations on paid Slack plans can enable data retention and compliance export policies that preserve messages and files independently of user-initiated deletion, and files under an active legal hold are typically retained regardless of what a user deletes from their own view.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can screenshots or PDF previews leak metadata even if I don't share the raw file?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">A plain image screenshot of a PDF page does not carry the PDF's Info dictionary or XMP fields, since those are file-format properties rather than visual content. However, the screenshot image itself can carry its own metadata, such as device or location data from a phone camera, and the visible page content in the screenshot can still expose the same information a full metadata field would.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <p>This article covers standard, unencrypted PDFs shared through Slack, Microsoft Teams, and conventional email as configured in their default enterprise settings. It does not cover organization-specific data loss prevention (DLP) rules, which some enterprise deployments layer on top of these platforms and which may scan or block files based on content patterns independently of anything discussed here. It also does not address end-to-end encrypted messaging platforms, which handle file storage differently, or files shared through platforms with server-side content scanning that could alter or flag files before delivery. Retention, export, and legal hold behavior varies by plan tier and by each organization's specific configuration — check with your IT or legal team for how your workspace is actually set up.</p>

    <p class="legal-note">This article is provided for general informational purposes and does not constitute legal or security compliance advice. Data handling obligations vary by industry, jurisdiction, and internal policy. Consult your organization's IT security and legal teams for guidance specific to your compliance requirements.</p>

